#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:38
msgid "Background"
msgstr "背景"

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:40
msgid ""
"A force along some direction here is defined as the negative gradient of the"
" potential energy along that direction."
msgstr ""

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:42
msgid ""
"A constant force $F$ on a scalar collective variable $Q(\\vec{X})$ therefore"
" is a simple addition to the system's energy function."
msgstr ""

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:48
msgid ""
"Notice that, because of the negative sign, a postive value of $F$ results in"
" a lower energy for large values of $Q$, meaning $F\\gt0$ corresponds to a "
"\"pulling\" force."
msgstr ""

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:50
msgid ""
"A mechanical force would often in reality would correspond to pulling apart "
"two atoms, and so $Q$ would often be a simple distance coordinate."
msgstr ""

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:52
msgid ""
"Note however, that other quantities could be used, such as an area which "
"would mean $F$ corresponds to a pressure."
msgstr ""

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:54
msgid ""
"Dimensional analysis implies that the units of $F$ must be [Energy]/[Q]."
msgstr ""

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:56
msgid ""
"The effect of constant force can be assessed using any enhanced sampling "
"method."
msgstr ""

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:58
msgid "The work done by the bias in this case is very simple"
msgstr ""

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:64
msgid ""
"Constant forces can be applied in PLUMED with the SLOPE keyword of the "
"RESTRAINT bias."
msgstr ""

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:66
msgid "Steered MD"
msgstr ""

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:68
msgid ""
"Steered molecular dynamics (SMD) is one way of pulling on a molecular "
"coordinate, and has a connection to experiments done where a molecule is "
"attached via a \"spring\" to an object such as an optical tweezer or AFM "
"tip. To represent this in simulation, instead of applying a constant force, "
"we impose a Harmonic restraint on $Q$ with a center that moves:"
msgstr ""

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:74
msgid ""
"Typically $Q_0(t)$ would move linearly, with $ Q_0(t) = Q_0(0)-\\lambda t $ "
"although that is not a requirement."
msgstr ""

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:76
msgid ""
"At any given time, the force along $Q$ from the moving bias is given as:"
msgstr ""

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:82
msgid ""
"This force is positive (pulling) when $Q_0(t)$ is bigger than $Q$, and it "
"can get very large if the spring moves quickly to larger values."
msgstr ""

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:84
msgid ""
"SMD is implemented in PLUMED using the MOVINGRESTRAINT bias, and the work is"
" computed automatically."
msgstr ""

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:94
msgid "FISST"
msgstr ""

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:96
msgid ""
"Infinite Switch Simulated Tempering in Force (FISST) is a method implemented"
" in [this paper](https://doi.org/10.1063/5.0009280) and demonstrated to be "
"compatible with replica exchange approaches in [this "
"paper](https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.jpcb.3c07081)"
msgstr ""

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:98
msgid ""
"This method takes advantage of the limit of changing a force from $F_{min}$ "
"to $F_{max}$ and back infinitely quickly."
msgstr ""

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:100
msgid ""
"In this limit, the system feels an average force $\\bar{F}(Q)$ that depends "
"on the current value of the pulling coordinate."
msgstr ""

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:106
msgid ""
"In practice, this $\\bar{F}(Q)$ is computed using weights $w(F)$ for each "
"force in the force range that are learned on the fly, and separate "
"\"observable weights\" $W_F(Q_i)$ are used to compute the average of any "
"quantity $A$ at a given force."
msgstr ""

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:112
msgid "where the observable weights are computed in a way that"
msgstr ""

#: INSTRUCTIONS.md:118
msgid ""
"The module writes out both the force weights and the observable weights to "
"files."
msgstr ""

